Umbra MAP Page Pool and Disk Root
=================================

Umbra stores logical-to-physical translations in a relation-local private MAP
fork.  Block 0 is a versioned, CRC-protected relation root.  The remaining
blocks are ordinary MAP pages served by the small shared pool in this
directory.  `ummap.c` owns both disk formats and interprets their contents;
the ordinary page pool owns residency, concurrency, and writeback for whole
ordinary MAP pages.  A separate relation-keyed cache owns root residency,
content locking, dirty state, and writeback ordering without interpreting the
root bytes.  The cache stores the 64-byte root payload; `ummap.c` transfers a
zero-padded 512-byte sector while retaining BLCKSZ addressing for later MAP
pages.

The pool deliberately follows the core ownership and replacement rules of the
PostgreSQL shared buffer pool.  It is not a second general-purpose buffer
manager: it caches only Umbra MAP metadata, not relation data pages.


Tags, Pins, and Content Locks
-----------------------------

Each pool entry is tagged with a RelFileLocatorBackend and a MAP block number.
A partitioned mapping lock protects the tag-to-slot hash table.  A lookup that
finds a tag increments the slot's pin count before releasing that mapping lock.
Once pinned, the slot cannot be recycled for another MAP page.  This is the
same handoff rule used by shared buffers.

A pin protects the slot identity and page memory.  The content lock protects
the page contents.  Callers hold a pin and a shared content lock to inspect
entries, or an exclusive content lock to modify them.  Pins and input I/O
ownership are registered with the current ResourceOwner, so ERROR and
subtransaction cleanup cannot leave a slot pinned or an input I/O owner
behind.

Input publishes a tagged slot as I/O in progress before reading it.  The slot
becomes valid only after the read completes.  A failed input removes the hash
entry and tag before the slot can be reused.  Thus a hash lookup never grants
access to a page whose input failed.


Replacement and Writeback
-------------------------

The pool has a freelist and a clock sweep.  A clock candidate is claimed by
atomically changing its pin count from zero to one before it is returned to a
caller.  A dirty victim is written before its tag can be reused.  The old and
new mapping partitions are locked in partition-number order while a tag is
replaced.

Output holds the I/O and exclusive content locks.  A successful write clears
the dirty state.  An error leaves the page dirty for a later retry and releases
the locks through the normal ERROR path.


MAP Page Format
---------------

An ordinary MAP entry stores the exact physical block for one logical block.
An entry without a mapping is InvalidBlockNumber.  When the MAP fork grows,
every new ordinary MAP page, including an intermediate gap page, is written as
0xFF.  Consequently an all-zero ordinary MAP page is corruption, not an
alternative representation of an unmapped page.


Disk Root Authority
-------------------

The root stores the authoritative normal-runtime logical EOF, a physical
allocation frontier, and an independently monotonic physical capacity for each
tracked fork.  The frontier names the next unclaimed P and can advance before
capacity is materialized.  Capacity says only how far the data fork is
addressable; it neither reserves P nor authorizes an L -> P translation.  The
ordinary MAP entry remains the sole authority for each exact mapping.

Normal relation open makes the root resident.  A miss reads and validates one
512-byte sector.  File growth beyond persisted capacity is conservatively
treated as already allocated, while file growth covered by persisted capacity
does not move the allocation frontier.  The cache publishes only the opaque
64-byte payload.  An update refreshes the payload CRC while holding the entry
content lock and marks the entry dirty; it does not immediately rewrite the
sector.

During normal execution, smgrnblocks reads the resident logical EOF directly.
The same backend can extend that value with its own contiguous unpublished
range.  It never waits for another backend's pending range.  Buffered zero
extension emits its structural mapping WAL and publishes MAP plus root before
releasing PostgreSQL's relation extension lock, so a later extender observes
the new resident EOF.  MAP entries above that EOF are otherwise unreachable
allocation or abort orphans until a later valid publication reaches them.

A missing MAP entry below a normal root EOF is corruption.  Normal execution
does not infer L -> L from the root.

Recovery does not use logical EOF to decide whether a WAL target exists or which
physical block it names.  It scans canonical and scratch mappings, uses the
exact P recorded in WAL, and installs record-scoped exact or scratch barriers.
Successful redo updates the root as an output of replay; recovery scratch never
advances it.


Mapping Publication
-------------------

Extension publishes the exact contiguous L -> P run selected by the storage
manager.  Normal publication can fill the current logical EOF or repeat an
existing mapping, but cannot replace a visible mapping.  Redo installs the
exact P recorded in WAL; it never repairs a missing entry by guessing L -> L.
Truncation clears every entry in [new logical EOF, old logical EOF), so a later
extension cannot make an old tail visible again merely by filling its first
entry.

Before WAL, a first-born range owns its ordinary MAP pins and a shared
visibility barrier.  After XLogInsertRecord returns an EndRecPtr, the critical
publication path marks already pinned MAP entries canonical and advances an
already-resident root.  It cannot allocate, attach shared state, or perform file
I/O.  Once MAP and root are published, it clears the pending bits and releases
the pins and barrier.

Buffered zero extension for MAIN, FSM, and VM attaches that range to a small
SMGR record with no page image and completes publication inside the storage
callback.  This is required before the relation extension lock is released,
and FSM may have no later page WAL at all.  Redo gives this record exclusive
ownership of the zero baseline: it locks and clears the logical shared buffers,
overwrites the exact target P range, and publishes the mapping only at record
end.  Direct MAIN extension may instead attach its already-materialized range
to the page WAL that initializes it.

First-born XLOG_FPI records can omit all-zero pages from MAIN, FSM, or VM while
one mapping header still owns the complete range.  Redo therefore establishes
the same complete zero baseline for every mapped fork before restoring the
listed images.  This also makes repeated recovery discard page state left by
WAL records later than that first-born record.

UmMappingPublicationDone runs outside a critical section.  It completes any MAP
publication that first required file I/O, advances the resident root while the
shared MAP barrier is still visible, and only then releases that barrier and the
checkpoint delay.  Publication therefore makes the canonical MAP and
authoritative resident EOF visible as one protected storage transition; disk
writeback remains deferred.


WAL Ordering
------------

Each descriptor retains the greatest WAL LSN associated with its unflushed
MAP changes.  Before writing a dirty MAP page, the pool calls XLogFlush() for
that LSN.  Successful output clears both the dirty state and the retained LSN;
failed output leaves both available for retry.  Changes that have no WAL
dependency use InvalidXLogRecPtr.

Each dirty root entry likewise retains the greatest WAL LSN associated with
its unflushed mapping changes.  A next-free reservation has no WAL dependency
because it publishes no mapping and may safely persist as an allocation hole.
Publication updates the resident image but does not flush WAL or write the root
sector.  Checkpoint or relation sync flushes any retained LSN before writing the
latest 512-byte root sector.  Successful output clears the dirty state and
watermark; failed output leaves both for retry.

DELAY_CHKPT_START covers the WAL-to-canonical-MAP-to-resident-root publication
window.  It is released only after the pending list is empty.  Dirty MAP pages
and roots retain their own WAL dependencies, so later checkpoint writeback
performs the required XLogFlush().

Before WAL insertion, transaction or subtransaction abort can discard a
pending mapping and leave an unreachable physical orphan or an unmaterialized
allocation hole.  Once a pending range has an EndRecPtr, publication leaves both
its MAP and resident EOF in place.  This matches PostgreSQL relation-extension
behavior: tuple visibility rolls back, storage extension does not.  Abort
cleanup never attempts root I/O.


Root Cache Locking
------------------

The root cache has its own dshash mapping lock and a content lock in each
relation entry.  Lookup acquires the content lock before releasing the dshash
lock, so invalidation cannot free an entry while a caller uses it.  Shared
content locking protects reads; exclusive locking serializes frontier updates,
load, flush, and invalidation.

Root output never runs while an ordinary MAP descriptor content, I/O, or
mapping-partition lock is held.  Critical publication may update an
already-resident root after releasing the MAP content lock, but it cannot attach
the root DSA, create a root entry, or load the root from disk.  A normal size
lookup reads the authoritative EOF while holding the root content lock.

Root bootstrap writes its 512-byte sector under the relation-keyed MAP extension
lock, then extends the file to preserve one BLCKSZ root slot.  The cache is used
only after block 0 exists.


Checkpoint Start Barrier
------------------------

WAL-backed mapping publication uses PostgreSQL's DELAY_CHKPT_START flag to keep
checkpoint start out of the interval between mapping WAL insertion and
checkpoint-visible MAP state.  If a checkpoint observes the backend before the
flag is set, the mapping WAL has not been inserted and will follow that
checkpoint's redo point.  If it observes the flag, it waits.  After the flag is
cleared, the existing publication path has made the canonical MAP entry visible
and dirty before checkpoint writeback begins.

The barrier does not define or validate the WAL payload, MAP publication, redo,
or root durability protocols.  Those are preconditions owned by their existing
paths.  It also does not wait for MAP fsync; checkpoint writeback and the WAL
flush frontier above own persistence after the MAP state is visible.


Relation Generations
--------------------

The root records the EndRecPtr of the MAIN CREATE record as the storage
generation identity.  Normal CREATE publishes that marker inside its checkpoint
delay.  Redo writes and syncs a replayed marker immediately so another crash can
distinguish the restored generation.

A second recovery can begin before an old generation's DROP while newer files
already occupy the same locator.  Before resolving a page or exact mapping,
redo compares its record position with the resident generation marker.  A
future marker causes the newer canonical files to be replaced by an empty
recovery scratch generation.  Missing pre-CREATE pages then use PostgreSQL's
invalid-page accounting until the old DROP or TRUNCATE record proves that the
dependency is disposable.  Redo CREATE installs the new generation marker and
subsequent exact mappings rebuild its storage.


Physical Segment Reclaim
------------------------

Truncating a MAP entry records its old physical segment as a reclaim candidate
before the reference disappears.  Existing-page remap publication instead
runs inside WAL insertion's critical section, where it may only re-age an
existing hash entry without allocation; the compactor's complete MAP scan
rediscovers a candidate that was not already present.
Candidates are deduplicated by relation, fork, and segment in a dynamic shared
hash.  A candidate carries the immutable relation generation and the checkpoint
epoch in which it was retired or discovered; it is not authority to remove the
file.

The candidate hash is intentionally volatile.  While examining a resident
root, the compactor also builds a live-segment set from one complete MAP scan
and compares it with the existing sparse segment filenames.  An unreferenced
complete segment below the append-only frontier is rediscovered with the same
generation and enters the normal checkpoint-aging queue.  An incomplete scan
or a logical EOF change fails closed.  Rediscovery does not re-age an existing
candidate, so repeated compactor rounds cannot postpone it indefinitely.

A successful checkpoint only advances the completed reclaim epoch and wakes
the mapcompactor worker.  The worker processes a bounded candidate batch and
conditionally takes transaction-level database lifecycle and relation-storage
locks, followed by the relation MAP extension lock.  It then cancels a
generation mismatch and rechecks canonical MAP runs, shared pending ranges, and
active reservations.  First-born allocation registers its relation/fork marker
while still holding the extension lock and before advancing the physical
frontier;
the marker remains through transaction end.  Existing-page remap instead keeps
the extension lock from reservation until its shared pending range records both
source P and target P.  A live or reserved P re-ages the candidate, and lock
failure delays it until the next successful checkpoint.  Thus source-P needed
by checkpoint capture and unpublished allocation targets cannot be removed by
current-MAP observation alone, and optional scans or file errors cannot fail an
already completed checkpoint.

Only a complete segment below the append-only physical frontier can be
removed.  The segment containing the frontier remains present even when it has
no live MAP entries, because another backend may retain an FD and allocate a
later P in that same segment.

Reservation accounting is fail-closed.  If its bounded DSA cannot record a
marker, foreground allocation continues, but shared state disables physical
reclaim until restart.  Optional space recovery therefore cannot turn memory
pressure into a DML availability failure.

Segment unlink is a primary-only space optimization.  WAL and recovery never
depend on it, and this patch does not WAL-log reclaim for standby space parity.
A standby may retain historical dead segments.  After promotion, loading the
relation root lets the local compactor reconstruct those candidates.  There is
no global startup scan of roots that have not become resident.


Lifecycle
---------

The selected storage manager receives generic checkpoint and database lifecycle
events from core.  Umbra translates them into private MAP operations.  A
checkpoint flushes dirty ordinary MAP pages, dirty resident roots, and then
ordinary MAP pages again.  The second scan catches a non-WAL MAP update that
the first scan passed before its root was written; an update after root output
leaves the disk root behind and is safe.  Immediate and registered relation
syncs flush matching pages and roots before syncing their relation storage.
Relation, database, and tablespace invalidation discard matching page slots and
root entries before their files can be removed or copied.

Logical truncation lowers the root EOF and clears every stale MAP entry in the
truncated range.  Mapped MAIN, FSM, and VM storage remains append-only, so
truncation does not lower a physical frontier or remove an old physical tail.
Transaction and subtransaction abort do not roll back a published storage EOF.
DROP removes the entire private MAP fork, including its root.


Deliberate Limits
-----------------

The pool size is fixed at postmaster startup as max(16, NBuffers / 128).  It
has no access-strategy ring, local MAP buffer pool, or pin-count waiter.  The
map writer uses a bounded conditional clock scan; it does not add a separate
replacement policy.

The root cache uses a 2 MiB in-place DSA and can grow under the postmaster to a
hard 64 MiB limit.  It has no replacement policy: entries remain until relation
or database invalidation, and a new insertion reports an error if the limit is
reached.  This is an explicit RFC limitation rather than a claim of unbounded
relation cardinality.  Each entry stores only the opaque 64-byte root image;
disk root I/O transfers one zero-padded 512-byte sector.

The map writer preallocates permanent MAIN, FSM, and VM forks from independent
low-water, foreground hard-water, and batch settings.  Capacity preallocation
does not reserve a P or publish a mapping; allocation still advances each
fork's append-only physical frontier.

The optional map compactor counts live P references independently for MAIN,
FSM, and VM and relocates shared buffers from sparse extents while skipping the
newest live extent.  Both resident and cold pages pass through the PostgreSQL
shared buffer pool; the compactor never copies old-P directly.  Every fork uses
a normal buffer read because image-preserving relocation does not take over the
FSM or VM repair policy.  A validation error fails the move instead of
synthesizing a different authoritative image.  After taking a conditional
exclusive buffer lock, it rechecks old-P and emits a deliberate full-image remap
carrying the exact old-P/new-P transition.  The normal automatic data-WAL remap
remains MAIN-only.
Compactor work is bounded per round by relation visits and relocation count,
uses a bulk-read buffer strategy, and rotates the first fork after each complete
resident-root pass so a one-move budget cannot permanently starve FSM or VM.
Bounded reclaim and relocation run in one short worker transaction so
checkpoint-delay processing can see a valid VXID.  Collecting the resident root
snapshot still scans all resident roots.

The reclaim queue uses a fixed 512 KiB in-place DSA, and the worker examines at
most eight eligible tasks per round.  Exhaustion drops optional retirement work
or disables reclaim until restart; it never blocks foreground allocation and
never reuses physical block numbers.  CRC detects an invalid root but does not
provide a duplicate root or repair arbitrary storage corruption.
